<p>Immanuel Kant: Reason, Duty, and the Moral Law</p>
<p><img src="/phil2306/assets/images/bild-des-kant-denkmals-in-knigsberg-an-seinem-ersten-pla.jpg" alt="A black and white artwork depicting a European cathedral with a fountain and tree, surrounded by buildings on a cobblestone street in an urban setting." width="860" height="340" loading="lazy" class="page-hero-image"></p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Aquinas grounded morality in human nature. Kant rejects that whole strategy and tries to ground morality in pure reason, accessible to any rational creature whatsoever.</p>
</div></div>
<div class="accordion">
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">1</span>
    What this unit is about
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Both of our previous thinkers grounded morality in what we are. For Aristotle, the good life is the life that fulfills the distinctive function of a human being. For Aquinas, the natural law is what reason discovers about the kind of creatures God made us to be. In both cases, facts about human nature do the moral work.</p>
<p>Kant is going to reject this strategy completely. In his view, building ethics on human nature makes morality contingent: if human nature were different, morality would be different. Kant thinks genuine moral principles cannot depend on what kind of creatures happen to exist. They must be binding on every rational being as such, from us to an angel to a Martian, if any such exists. That means moral principles have to come from reason alone.</p>
<p>The <em>Groundwork for the Metaphysic of Morals</em> is Kant's attempt to show what such a morality would look like and why we are bound by it. The book is short: around 40 pages of text, structured as a Preface and three sections. But it is one of the most difficult books you will read in this course, and it has shaped ethics more deeply than almost any other work since Aristotle.</p>
<p>The core idea is the <em>categorical imperative</em>: a moral principle that holds unconditionally, regardless of what we want or what consequences might follow. Kant argues that there is exactly one such principle at the foundation of morality, and he gives us several different formulations of it. The most famous: act only on a principle you could will to be a universal law. Another: never treat other people merely as means to your ends, but always also as ends in themselves.</p>
<div class="callout callout--connection"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">➡️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>← LOOKING BACK &nbsp;|&nbsp; LOOKING AHEAD →</p>
<p>Aquinas's natural law took facts about human nature (we are rational animals inclined toward certain goods) and derived moral precepts from them. We flagged this as the is/ought move: deriving "ought" from "is." Kant saw the same gap and drew the opposite conclusion. If morality cannot be derived from facts about what we are, he reasoned, then morality must come from somewhere else entirely. His answer: pure practical reason. Unit 4 will bring Mill, who rejects both Aquinas and Kant and tries to ground morality in consequences.</p>
</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">2</span>
    Before you begin
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Kant is not easy. His prose is dense, his vocabulary is technical, and he builds arguments in long chains where one misstep early makes later sections hard to follow. Students who come in expecting to read Kant the way they read a novel usually bounce off the text.</p>
<p>There is good news. The Groundwork is a short book, and its argument, though elaborate, is reasonably linear. If you read the first section carefully, the second and third become much more tractable. The edition we will use is a modern English translation by Jonathan Bennett, which renders Kant's German in the clearest English available without sacrificing the substance of the argument. Bennett marks his own explanatory additions with dots and brackets, so you can always see what is Kant and what is editorial help.</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>READING STRATEGY</p>
<p>Plan to read each section twice. The first pass is for orientation: read through without stopping, even when paragraphs feel impenetrable. The second pass is for understanding: go back to the passages that mattered most and work through them slowly. This is how most philosophers read Kant, and it works.</p>
<p>If a passage still seems opaque after a second reading, flag it and move on. Kant builds arguments that clarify earlier passages retrospectively. Sometimes you only understand a line from the First Section after you have finished the Second.</p>
</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">3</span>
    Learning objectives
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>By the end of this unit, you should be able to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Explain why Kant rejects ethics grounded in human nature, human happiness, or the consequences of action.</li>
<li>Define the good will and explain why Kant holds that it is the only unconditional good.</li>
<li>Distinguish hypothetical from categorical imperatives and give examples of each.</li>
<li>State the universalization formulation of the categorical imperative and apply it to a concrete case.</li>
<li>State the humanity-as-end formulation of the categorical imperative and explain what it means to treat someone merely as a means.</li>
<li>Explain the connection, in Kant's view, between freedom, autonomy, and the moral law.</li>
</ul>
