<p>All of our Kant reading comes from a single text: the <em>Groundwork for the Metaphysic of Morals</em>, in Jonathan Bennett's modern English translation. The full text runs about 40 pages and is free to read online.</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://www.earlymoderntexts.com/assets/pdfs/kant1785.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>Kant, <em>Groundwork for the Metaphysic of Morals</em></strong></a> (1785), translated by Jonathan Bennett</li>
</ul>
<p>We will take the text in order, one section per lecture page.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Preface and Chapter 1</strong>: common morality, the good will, duty (paired with the Good Will and Duty lecture)</li>
<li><strong>Chapter 2</strong>: hypothetical and categorical imperatives, the formulations (paired with the Categorical Imperative lecture)</li>
<li><strong>Chapter 3</strong>: freedom, autonomy, the two standpoints (paired with the Freedom and Autonomy lecture)</li>
</ul>
